Reactivity Transform (Vue 3.2 experimental) let you write `let count = $ref(0)` and use `count` without `.value`, with `$computed` and `$$()` to get the underlying ref. It was opt-in via `reactivityTransform:true`.

Vue team removed it in 3.4 after RFC discussion: it created two dialects of Vue, broke IDE/TS intellisense, made `ref` vs raw values ambiguous, and complicated compilation. The maintenance cost outweighed saving `.value`.

Migration is mechanical: rename `$ref`→`ref`, add `.value` where read/written in script, and remove `$$()`. The VueUse `toRefs` and `refAutoReset` helpers remain valid. Going forward, use plain `ref` and rely on template auto-unwrapping only.

| Before ($ref) | After (3.4) |
|---|---|
| `let x = $ref(0)` / use `x++` | `const x = ref(0)` / use `x.value++` |
| `$$ (x)` to get ref | just `x` is the ref |
| `defineExpose({x})` leaked impl | explicit `ref` is clearer for tools |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I migrated by removing reactivityTransform from vite.config and running the codemod `npx vue-codemod reactivity-transform` — the explicit .value is one model to learn.'

```vue
// before (removed)
let count = $ref(0)
function inc(){ count++ }

// after — Vue 3.4 canonical
import { ref } from 'vue'
const count = ref(0)
function inc(){ count.value++ }

// template still auto-unwraps — no .value needed
// <template>{{ count }}</template>
```

**TL;DR:** Shallow refs track only .value replacement, not deep mutations — use for large datasets + triggerRef() to force updates.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Shallow: top-level tracking only → manual trigger if mutated</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">deep ref</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">proxies every nested</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">costly for large data</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">auto deep</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#8b5cf6">shallowRef</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">only .value tracked</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">mutations invisible</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">fast</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">triggerRef</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">force update</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">after manual mutate</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">explicit</text></svg></div>

`ref`/`reactive` deeply proxy objects, which is expensive for a 10k-row dataset or a Three.js scene graph. `shallowRef` and `shallowReactive` only track the top-level reference / property assignment: `r.value = newObj` triggers, but `r.value.nested = 1` does not.

Use shallow for large immutable-style data you replace wholesale (e.g. fetched table rows). If you must mutate in place, call `triggerRef(r)` to manually notify watchers. `shallowReactive` similarly tracks only first-level property sets.

`markRaw` and `readonly` complement this: `markRaw(largeLibObj)` opts out entirely, while shallow keeps swap-level reactivity.

| API | Depth | When |
|---|---|---|
| `ref` / `reactive` | deep proxy | typical UI state |
| `shallowRef` | top-level `.value` only | large list / canvas / charts |
| `triggerRef(r)` | manual notify | after in-place mutation of shallow |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'For a 20k-row virtual table I store rows in shallowRef and replace the array on fetch; if I edit a cell in place I call triggerRef to avoid deep proxy cost.'

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
import { shallowRef, triggerRef } from 'vue'
type Row = { id:number, v:string }
const rows = shallowRef<Row[]>([])
async function load(){ rows.value = await fetch('/api/rows').then(r=>r.json()) }
// in-place mutate needs manual trigger
function patch(id:number, v:string){
  const r = rows.value.find(x=>x.id===id)
  if(r){ r.v = v; triggerRef(rows) }
}
</script>
<template><div v-for="r in rows" :key="r.id">{{ r.v }}</div></template>
```

**TL;DR:** effectScope groups reactive effects so they can be stopped together — essential for composables outside component lifecycle.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">effectScope bundles watches/effects for bulk stop</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#ef4444"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#ef4444">Without scope</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">watch per call</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">manual stop handle</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">leaks if forgotten</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#8b5cf6">effectScope()</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">scope.run(()=>watch...)</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">collects effects</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">scope.stop() all</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Use case</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">composable re-use</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">SSR cleanup</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">pause/resume</text></svg></div>

`effectScope()` creates a scope that captures any `watch`, `computed`, or `effect` created inside `scope.run()`. Calling `scope.stop()` stops all captured effects at once. Components already create an implicit scope that stops on unmount, but composables used outside a component (router guards, pinia stores, `createApp` plugins) need explicit scope management.

Example: `useMouse()` that watches `x`/`y` inside a scope can be paused/resumed via `scope.pause()` / `scope.resume()` (Vue 3.5). Without scope each `watch` returns a stop handle you must track manually.

Vue's `getCurrentScope()` and `onScopeDispose(fn)` let libraries hook disposal.

| Pattern | Cleanup |
|---|---|
| Component `watch` | auto on unmount (implicit scope) |
| Outside component | `const s=effectScope(); s.run(()=>watch(...)); s.stop()` |
| Pause | `s.pause()` / `s.resume()` (3.5) |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'When I write a composable that may run outside setup, I wrap watches in effectScope so the consumer can dispose the whole group with one call.'

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
import { effectScope, ref, watch } from 'vue'

function usePoller(cb:()=>void){
  const scope = effectScope()
  scope.run(()=>{
    watch(() => Date.now(), cb)
    // more watches...
  })
  function stop(){ scope.stop() }
  function pause(){ scope.pause() }
  return { stop, pause, resume: ()=> scope.resume() }
}

const poller = usePoller(()=> console.log('tick'))
// later: poller.stop() stops all
</script>
```
